.TH ORBITALTEST 1
.SH NAME
orbitaltest \- check orbital(2)'s hydrogen wave functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B orbitaltest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the functions
.IR danby-orbital (1)
draws, not a copy of them, so a shape that would come out wrong on
screen fails here first.
.PP
The checks are the ones that DEFINE the wave functions rather than
describe them.  Each is normalised; states differing in any quantum
number are orthogonal; the radial part has exactly n\-l\-1 nodes; psi at
the nucleus is 1/sqrt(pi) for the ground state and exactly zero for
every state with angular momentum.  A set of formulas satisfying all of
those IS the hydrogen wave function, so this is a definition and not a
sample of values.
.PP
The integrals are done separately in r and over the sphere rather than
on a three-dimensional grid.  The product of the two normalisations is
the one that matters, and doing it this way is both far cheaper and far
more accurate than any grid a test could afford.
.SH ORIENTATION
Every integral above is blind to which way round an orbital points: an
integral over all space cannot tell p_x from p_y, and swapping them
passes every one.  So the orientations are checked directly \(em p_z
largest along z and zero in the xy plane, p_x the other way about, p_y a
quarter turn from p_x \(em and so are the signs, since the (\-1)^m phase
that puts p_x's positive lobe at positive x is exactly the sort of
factor that can be dropped without any integral noticing.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/orbitaltest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR orbital (2),
.IR danby-orbital (1)
.SH BUGS
It checks the functions, not the picture.  Whether
.IR danby-orbital (1)
projects or colours them correctly is not tested here; its
.B -c
option reports the cloud it would draw, which is how that is checked by
hand.
